Below are the three safe and reliable strategies used in real performance-testing environments when SSO blocks JMeter recording.
I’ll explain each one clearly so you can choose the best approach for your application.
✅ 1. Record Everything After Logging In (Recommended for Most SSO Apps)
✔️ How it works
Open browser without JMeter proxy
Log in using SSO normally
Once you are in the application (already authenticated),
start the JMeter proxy recorder
Perform only the post-login actions (navigation, form actions, API calls)
✔️ Why it works
SSO authentication uses:
temporary tokens
redirects
strict certificate validation
Once authenticated:
JMeter can record application API calls (not SSO redirects)
You avoid the entire SSO flow
✔️ When to use
✔️ Most corporate SSO apps
✔️ Azure AD / OKTA / PingFederate
✔️ Systems where login is not part of performance test scope
✅ 2. Ask DevOps / Backend Team for a "Bypass Login" API Token (Best for Large Organizations)
✔️ How it works
Many SSO-protected apps still have:
a backend login API
or a token-generation endpoint
You request the backend team for:
→ A static test token or test user with API login access
Then in JMeter:
You skip browser login completely
Use HTTP Request to authenticate using:
token
session cookie
or JWT
✔️ Why it works
SSO is for browser login.
Performance testing does not need to authenticate using the UI.
✔️ Typical examples
✔️ Get a JWT token by sending username/password to /auth/login
✔️ Get access token using OAuth client credentials
✔️ Use special header like x-api-key: <token>
If your app supports this, it is the cleanest and most stable solution.
✅ 3. Use a Pre-Recorded Token or Cookie in JMeter (Advanced but Reliable)
✔️ How it works
Log in to the application manually
Copy the session cookie (e.g., JSESSIONID, AUTH_TOKEN, SSO_TOKEN)
Inject this cookie into JMeter using HTTP Cookie Manager
Directly start hitting application API endpoints
✔️ Why this works
SSO provides session cookies that JMeter can reuse.
✔️ Caveat
Many SSO tokens expire every:
30 minutes
1 hour
24 hours
So this solution is good for short test sessions.
❌ What NOT to do
These will not work reliably with SSO:
✗ Trying to record the SSO login screen
✗ Capturing redirects from Azure/OKTA (blocked by certificate rules)
✗ Running corporate SSO through JMeter proxy
SSO security is specifically designed to block MITM proxies, including JMeter.
